-- title:
--   Height-one prime containing p, avoiding q and the node
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, $A$ a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $k$ a field of characteristic $q$ and $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$ a ring homomorphism whose kernel is exactly the maximal ideal of $A$. Let $a\in k$ satisfy: every elliptic curve over $k$ with $j$-invariant $a$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$ (the condition `ssJSet`), $a^{q^{2}}=a$, $a\neq 0$ and $a\neq 1728$. Let $f$ belong to the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the full modular function field of level $1\cdot q$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}((X))$, and assume: $f$ lies in the localisation `modularLocalized` of the modular ring of level $1\cdot q$ over $A$ at the complement of the kernel of reduction, its reduction under `modularRedLocHom` lies in the subfield of $k((X))$ generated over $k$ by $j$ and $j_{q}$ and is nonzero, the Fricke involute of $f$ also lies in that localisation with nonzero reduction, and the reduction of $f$ lies in the valuation ring of the first place of `frobNodePair q a` (the place attached to the point $a$) without being a unit there. Let $K$ be a number field inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $f\in$ `fieldOver (1*q) K`, the subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((X))$ generated by constants from $K$ together with $j$ and $j_{q}$. Let $A_{0}=A\cap K$, let $x\in A_{0}$ reduce to $a$, and let $\varpi\in A_{0}$ generate the kernel of $A_{0}\to k$. Finally let $p,s$ lie in the integral closure $C$ of the ring $jRing\,A\,K$ inside `fieldOver (1*q) K`, with $s$ the evaluation at $(j,j_{q})$ of a two-variable polynomial $s_{0}$ over $A_{0}$ whose value at $(a,a^{q})$ is nonzero, and with $f\cdot s=p$. Then there is a prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $C$ of height $1$ with $q\notin\mathfrak p$, $p\in\mathfrak p$, and such that for every two-variable polynomial $t$ over $A_{0}$ with value at $(a,a^{q})$ nonzero, if the evaluation of $t$ at $(j,j_{q})$ lies in $C$ then that element is not in $\mathfrak p$.
--
--   This is the step that produces, from a modular function $f$ whose reduction vanishes at the supersingular node $(a,a^{q})$, a height-one prime of the normalisation of $A_{0}[j,j_{q}]$ through which the vanishing can be measured, all polynomial expressions in $j,j_{q}$ non-vanishing at the node remaining outside it. It is the form of `exists_isPrime_mem_of_mul_eq_of_not_isUnit_frobNodePair` with the restriction $5\le q$ removed, the cases $q=2,3$ being excluded by $a\neq 0$ together with the vanishing of supersingular $j$-invariants in those characteristics; it feeds the subsequent computations of centred orders at the node.
